  "text": "Allophone: A variant form of a phoneme that occurs in a specific phonetic environment.\n\nAnaphora: The use of a word or phrase that refers back to a previously mentioned word or phrase.\n\nApophony: A change in sound that accompanies a change in grammatical function, such as a shift in vowel quality in nouns and verbs.\n\nArticulatory phonetics: The study of how speech sounds are produced by the vocal tract.\n\nBilingualism: The ability to speak and understand two languages fluently.\n\nChild-directed speech: A simplified and expressive way of speaking that adults use when interacting with children.\n\nClitic: A word that is grammatically dependent on another word but phonologically independent.\n\nComplement: A phrase or word that completes the meaning of another word, typically a verb or noun.\n\nConsonant: A speech sound produced by blocking or restricting the airflow in the vocal tract.\n\nConsonant cluster: A sequence of two or more consonants that appear together without a vowel between them.\n\nCopula: A verb that links the subject to a predicate, such as \"be\" in English.\n\nDialect: A variety of a language that is characteristic of a particular group or region.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound that begins with one vowel quality and glides to another.\n\nDissimilation: A phonological process in which sounds become more different from each other.\n\nDialectometry: The study of dialect variation through statistical analysis of language data.\n\nDislocated subject: A subject that is not in the expected position in a sentence.\n\nDuality of patterning: The property of human language where a finite number of sounds can be combined in an infinite number of ways.\n\nDyadic communication: Communication between two individuals, typically involving a sender and a receiver.\n\nEuphony: The quality of a word or phrase that is pleasant or harmonious to the ear.\n\nEuphemism: A mild or indirect word or phrase used to replace a harsher or more direct one.\n\nFeminine: A grammatical category used to classify nouns, pronouns, and adjectives referring to females.\n\nFocalization: The process of emphasizing a particular word or phrase in a sentence.\n\nFormant: A resonance frequency of the vocal tract that contributes to the quality of a vowel sound.\n\nFree morpheme: A morpheme that can stand alone as a word.\n\nFricative: A consonant sound produced by turbulent airflow through a narrow channel.\n\nGender: A grammatical category that classifies nouns, pronouns, and adjectives.\n\nGesture: A movement of the hands, face, or body that accompanies speech and conveys meaning.\n\nGlottal stop: A consonant sound produced by closing the vocal folds briefly.\n\nGradedness: The degree to which a phoneme can be substituted with another without causing a change in meaning.\n\nGrammatical category: A class of linguistic elements that includes words or phrases according to their function in a sentence.\n\nGrammaticalization: The process by which lexical items become grammatical elements.\n\nGrapheme: A written symbol that represents a phoneme or a set of phonemes.\n\nGrammatization: The process by which inflectional markers and derivational affixes become more abstract.\n\nHalpern's Law: A principle in sociolinguistics that states that language change is gradual and occurs uniformly across a speech community.\n\nHigh vowel: A vowel produced with the tongue high in the mouth.\n\nHindrance: A phonological process that involves the reduction or alteration of sounds due to articulatory difficulty.\n\nHomorganic: Sounds that are produced with the same place of articulation.\n\nHyponymy: A relationship in which one word is a specific instance of another word.\n\nIdiom: A group of words that expresses a meaning that cannot be predicted",
